#!/bin/sh
#
# ARM 1B — THE ROADS `pre-commit` NEVER SEES (#611, the law-7 sibling of
# #606/#519).
#
# `.githooks/pre-commit` ARM 1 refuses a commit made in the MAIN working tree
# while HEAD is a `team/*` branch (docs/RETRO_LOG.md R3: the founder's
# interactive sessions and the night shift share one working directory, and a
# commit made there on a shift branch has swept up another seat's work more
# than once). PR #610 measured that git does not run `pre-commit` AT ALL on
# three roads — `git revert`, `git cherry-pick` and a REPLAYED rebase commit —
# so ARM 1 is simply absent on them. Under deploy-on-merge every rollback proof
# is a revert, which makes the commonest of the three a road shifts walk ON
# PURPOSE. This hook is that arm's backstop.
#
# ⚠ WHY HERE, AND WHY THAT IS NOT WHAT #611 RECOMMENDED. The card ruled
# `prepare-commit-msg` out and recommended a `post-checkout` arm. Both halves
# of that were re-driven on git 2.55.0.windows.3 before this was written, one
# scratch repository per hook per road, each hook installed ALONE and exiting
# 1 — and the verdict read at the BRANCH REF, never at HEAD:
#
#                          ordinary   revert    cherry-pick  rebase replay
#   pre-commit             BLOCKED    committed committed    replayed
#   prepare-commit-msg     BLOCKED    BLOCKED   BLOCKED      BLOCKED
#   post-checkout          committed  committed committed    replayed
#   reference-transaction  BLOCKED    BLOCKED   BLOCKED      BLOCKED
#
#   - `post-checkout` exits 1 and the branch switches anyway: git documents its
#     exit code as ignored, so it CANNOT refuse. That is the same property the
#     card correctly used to rule out `post-commit`, and it rules out the
#     option the card recommended.
#     ⚠ If you re-run that row, read the OUTCOME and not git's exit code: git
#     reports non-zero here on Windows and ZERO on ubuntu after the same failing
#     hook. The branch switches on both, which is the finding; the code is a
#     platform quirk that reads like one.
#   - The card's objection to `prepare-commit-msg` was about a hook that WRITES
#     AND STAGES A FILE (#610 drove that: the staged file is dropped in a
#     revert). A REFUSAL has no such problem — firing twice in a revert simply
#     refuses twice — and it covers all four roads.
#   - `reference-transaction` also covers all four, and was built and driven
#     first. It was DISCARDED ON COST: it fires on every ref update in the
#     repository, measured at SEVEN invocations per commit and FOURTEEN per
#     checkout, adding ~137 ms to every commit, checkout, fetch, reset and
#     stash in the founder's own sessions. This hook is ONE invocation per
#     commit and ZERO on checkout and fetch, for the same coverage.
#
# ⚠ THE ONE ROAD NEITHER THIS NOR `pre-commit` COVERS IS `git am`, AND IT IS
# DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED. Measured the same way: `git am`
# runs the `applypatch` hooks and not this one, so a mailed patch applied onto
# a `team/*` branch in the main tree commits with no refusal. It is not
# backstopped because no road in this repository walks it — the shift workflow
# is branch, PR, squash-merge, and `git am` appears nowhere in the standing
# orders, the rite or any script. `reference-transaction` WOULD cover it, at
# the cost above. If patches ever become a road here, that is the trade to
# re-open, and this paragraph is where to start.
#
# HEAD IS DETACHED DURING A REBASE, which is why this hook does not simply ask
# `git symbolic-ref` the way ARM 1 does: mid-replay that returns nothing at
# all, and "no branch" is a state ARM 1 deliberately allows. The branch being
# replayed onto is named in git's own state file for the duration, and that is
# the only place it is named.
#
# THE ESCAPE IS FIXING THE COMMIT, NOT --no-verify (which skips this hook
# exactly as it skips pre-commit). Driven by
# server/prepareCommitMsgGate.test.ts against real temporary repositories, in
# both directions, on every road in the table above.
#
# Installed by:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks

# The condition and the refusal are SOURCED, not written here — see
# .githooks/shift-branch-guard. ARM 1 reads the same two, so the rule cannot
# drift into being two rules.
. "$(dirname "$0")/shift-branch-guard"

# The ordinary road: HEAD is the branch being committed onto.
branch=$(git symbolic-ref --short -q HEAD 2>/dev/null)

# The replay road: HEAD is detached, and the branch is in git's state file.
# `rebase-merge` is the interactive/merge backend, `rebase-apply` the older
# am-based one; whichever exists names the branch as a full ref.
if [ -z "$branch" ]; then
  git_dir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null)
  if [ -n "$git_dir" ]; then
    for state in "$git_dir/rebase-merge/head-name" "$git_dir/rebase-apply/head-name"; do
      if [ -f "$state" ]; then
        head_name=$(cat "$state" 2>/dev/null)
        case "$head_name" in
          refs/heads/*) branch=${head_name#refs/heads/} ;;
        esac
      fi
    done
  fi
fi

case "$branch" in
  team/*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

drape_in_main_working_tree || exit 0

drape_refuse_shift_branch "$branch" "That commit"
echo "This is .githooks/prepare-commit-msg — git does not run pre-commit on a" >&2
echo "revert, a cherry-pick or a replayed rebase, so this arm is what caught it." >&2
echo "" >&2
exit 1
